// ex_txt -- the `id` source text that evaluates a C expression.
//
// Every emitted expression is a `word` holding the C value in one canonical
// form: a signed type narrower than 64 bits arrives sign-extended, an
// unsigned one zero-extended, an 8-byte type as its 64 raw bits. Holding to
// that everywhere is what lets the operators below be chosen from the type
// alone -- there is never a question of what state a subexpression left its
// value in.
//
// The dispatch is the same lazy chain as ex_ty's, and for the same reason.
//
// What does *not* live here: `&&`, `||` and `?:` short-circuit, so they are
// not expressions at all in the lowering -- they become extra basic blocks
// with a frame temporary, which only the CFG builder can make. They emit an
// identifier that no generated project defines, so a program that reaches one
// fails to compile rather than quietly computing the wrong thing.

ex_txt(int id) {
  string s = "";
  if(k_of(id) == "num") {
    s = num_txt(id);
  } else {
    s = ex2(id);
  }
} return string s;
given txt_fixture (0):("NEEDS_CFG_prim") then calls ex2:(0)
given txt_fixture (5):("NEEDS_CFG_ptr") then calls ex2:(5)

// The three lvalue kinds. All of them are "compute an address, then load it",
// which is one function; what differs between them is only how the address is
// built, which is ex/a/.
ex2(int id) {
  string s = "";
  if(k_of(id) == "var" || k_of(id) == "idx" || k_of(id) == "mem") {
    s = lval_txt(id);
  } else {
    s = ex3(id);
  }
} return string s;
given txt_fixture (0):("NEEDS_CFG_prim") then calls ex3:(0)
given txt_fixture (5):("NEEDS_CFG_ptr") then calls ex3:(5)

ex3(int id) {
  string s = "";
  if(k_of(id) == "bin") {
    s = bin_txt(id);
  } else {
    s = ex4(id);
  }
} return string s;
given txt_fixture (0):("NEEDS_CFG_prim") then calls ex4:(0)
given txt_fixture (5):("NEEDS_CFG_ptr") then calls ex4:(5)
